  "response_text": "__io_putchar pushes a character over the UART interface (using HAL_UART_Transmit), it then returns the same character. This method is used by printf() in order to help us send data over UART. This allows us to monitor variables and/or logs that the microcontroller has during runtime. Based off the fact that __io_putchar uses \u201cIO\u201d instead of \u201cUART\u201d, this method is probably overloaded depending on the target system in order to provide printf() or any other a method to output characters. For example, you might overload __io_putchar to push a character into stream instead of UART on your personal computer; it depends on the platform. The return type of __io_putchar is to just verify that it has succeeded, it is not the main reason the function exists and is for error checking.\\n",
